1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,220
Two modules in gloss care about the same thing: what the doc comments say.

2
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:07,830
The lint checks them against a house style.

3
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:09,950
The model reads them as contracts.

4
00:00:10,310 --> 00:00:13,550
If each one parsed the sources on its own, they could disagree.

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,720
So both start from one Reading, made by Gloss Read.

6
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:19,980
It sits at the bottom of the package.

7
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,900
It knows Swift's syntax and doc format, and nothing of rules or contracts.

8
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:27,980
It depends only on the swift syntax library.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,100
A Reading is made of declarations.

10
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:34,740
Each comes as an entry with three parts.

11
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:40,850
The first is the Declaration itself: its kind, its name, its parameters,

12
00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:42,570
and whether it returns anything.

13
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:48,070
It is plain data, kept apart from the syntax tree, so a rule can be tested

14
00:00:48,070 --> 00:00:48,830
from a literal.

15
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:54,420
The second is the Doc Comment, line by line, each with its source line number.

16
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,620
A Doc Structure then reads it as a summary, callouts, and notes.

17
00:00:59,342 --> 00:01:01,182
The third is the details.

18
00:01:01,462 --> 00:01:05,922
The access level, the declaration as written, and the type names it mentions.

19
00:01:06,282 --> 00:01:11,102
Each mention says where it sits: in a property, a parameter, a return,

20
00:01:11,502 --> 00:01:15,282
a throws clause, an inheritance clause, or only in a body.

21
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:21,020
The Reading also holds an index of everything the sources declare.

22
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,940
A link in one file is checked against all of them.

23
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:26,890
Objective-C headers ride along.

24
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:28,670
They are not parsed.

25
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:33,750
Only the type names they declare are read, so a link to one still resolves.

26
00:01:34,404 --> 00:01:39,404
And a module's page, the page in the module's folder, is read as a page,

27
00:01:39,404 --> 00:01:41,464
with the same rules as a doc comment.

28
00:01:41,924 --> 00:01:44,564
The lint can check it, and the app can lead with it.

29
00:01:45,314 --> 00:01:49,754
Reading spreads the Swift files over every core, then builds the index.

30
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:54,333
Sometimes the question is about an older version.

31
00:01:55,013 --> 00:02:00,193
Revision reads the same sources at any commit, straight from git, without checking anything out.

32
00:02:00,943 --> 00:02:05,583
It can also read from git's index: what the next commit will hold.

33
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:09,923
So a pre-commit hook judges the commit, not the working tree.

34
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:15,133
Because both sides are read the same way, a diff compares like with like.

35
00:02:15,493 --> 00:02:19,493
One reading, from any moment, for every other module to build on.
